\fs28\b 12. Email OTP Signup Verification\b0\fs22\par
Signup now uses a pending MongoDB registration and does not create a User or issue a JWT until the email code is verified. The frontend calls POST /api/auth/register, then displays /verify-email only after the backend has accepted the registration and Resend has accepted the email. POST /api/auth/verify-otp validates the four-digit code, expiration, single-use state, and attempt limit, then creates the user and returns the existing JWT session. POST /api/auth/resend-otp issues a replacement code only when the server-side resendAvailableAt has passed. The 60-second cooldown is atomic in MongoDB and is restored in the UI after refresh. Pending registrations expire automatically after 15 minutes.\par\par
\b Changed files\b0\par
backend/src/config/env.js, backend/src/controllers/authController.js, backend/src/models/PendingRegistration.js, backend/src/services/emailService.js, backend/src/routes/authRoutes.js, backend/.env.example, frontend/src/services/api.js, frontend/src/services/authService.js, frontend/src/context/AuthContext.jsx, frontend/src/pages/Auth/Register.jsx, frontend/src/pages/Auth/VerifyEmail.jsx, frontend/src/routes/AppRoutes.jsx.\par\par
\b Backend environment variables\b0\par
Add RESEND_API_KEY with the private Resend API key and RESEND_FROM_EMAIL with a sender permitted by Resend, for example Preply <onboarding@resend.dev> for development or a verified production domain sender. These variables belong only in the backend environment and must never use the frontend REACT_APP_ prefix or be committed. The existing PORT, MONGODB_URI, JWT_SECRET, and FRONTEND_URL remain required for the backend.\par\par
\b Local testing\b0\par
Copy the backend environment example, set RESEND_API_KEY and RESEND_FROM_EMAIL, start MongoDB, then run npm ci and npm start from backend. Start the frontend with npm ci and npm start from frontend. Register with a new email, enter the real four-digit Resend code, and confirm redirect to /interview/setup. Confirm an existing email is rejected, an incorrect or expired code is rejected, a code cannot be reused, resend is rejected during the 60-second countdown (including after refreshing /verify-email), and login still works for an existing user. Without valid Resend credentials and a permitted sender, registration intentionally returns a configuration or delivery error and does not activate an account.\par\par
\b Deployment steps and outstanding setup\b0\par
Deploy the backend changes to the separate Node host described in this guide, set RESEND_API_KEY and RESEND_FROM_EMAIL there, verify the sender/domain in Resend for production, and set the Vercel REACT_APP_API_URL to that backend HTTPS URL. Keep FRONTEND_URL set to the exact Vercel origin for CORS. No email delivery test can be claimed until valid Resend credentials, a permitted sender, reachable MongoDB, and a deployed backend are available.\par
\par
\fs28\b 13. Forgot Password Email OTP\b0\fs22\par
The login page now links to /forgot-password. Password recovery is a separate flow from signup and uses PasswordReset records in MongoDB. The frontend calls POST /api/auth/forgot-password, POST /api/auth/forgot-password/verify-otp, POST /api/auth/forgot-password/resend-otp, and POST /api/auth/forgot-password/reset. Reset OTPs are hashed, expire after 5 minutes, are limited to five attempts, are single-use, and use the same atomic 60-second server-side resend cooldown. After OTP verification, a new password is bcrypt-hashed and existing JWT sessions are invalidated through the user's sessionVersion.\par\par
\b Changed files\b0\par
backend/src/models/PasswordReset.js, backend/src/services/emailService.js, backend/src/config/env.js, backend/src/controllers/authController.js, backend/src/routes/authRoutes.js, backend/src/models/User.js, backend/src/middleware/authMiddleware.js, frontend/src/services/authService.js, frontend/src/pages/Auth/Login.jsx, frontend/src/pages/Auth/ForgotPassword.jsx, frontend/src/routes/AppRoutes.jsx.\par\par
\b Testing and deployment\b0\par
Use the existing RESEND_API_KEY and RESEND_FROM_EMAIL backend variables; no frontend secret is required. With MongoDB and Resend configured, request a reset, verify valid and invalid codes, wait for expiration, test the 60-second resend response, exceed the attempt limit, set a new password, confirm redirect to login, and confirm the old password and old authenticated JWT no longer work. The frontend and backend build/start checks remain the same as the signup OTP flow. Without valid Resend credentials and a permitted sender, reset requests still return the same generic response and do not modify passwords; the reset record is removed so no account can be changed without a delivered code.\par
